VALUES (
  '2026/10/15/prescribed-rate-seventh-quarter-at-3-percent',
  'tax',
  'article',
  'The Family-Loan Prescribed Rate Is Headed for a Seventh Quarter at 3%, and No October 28 Decision Can Change That',
  'Three-month bills sit 62 basis points below the 3.00% average needed to lift the first-quarter 2027 rate to 4%, which leaves December 31 and January 30 as the dates that matter.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The rate stays at 3% on the data,</strong><span> the 90-day bill yielded 2.38% on October 13, 62 basis points below the 3.00% October average that would lift the rate to 4%.</span></li>
<li><strong>A fall to 2% is further off in rate terms than a rise,</strong><span> it needs an average at or below 2.00%, which is 38 basis points lower.</span></li>
<li><strong>Six quarters at 3% so far,</strong><span> the CRA set October to December at 3% from July auctions that averaged 2.29%.</span></li>
<li><strong>December 31 is the lock date,</strong><span> a loan made by then keeps 3% for its life, while a later loan takes whatever the CRA sets for 2027.</span></li>
<li><strong>January 30, 2027 is the payment deadline,</strong><span> late interest attributes the income back to the lender for that year and every later year.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The CRA prescribed rate on loans to family members has been 3% for six consecutive quarters, and the data point to a seventh. Each quarter rate is the average yield on three-month Government of Canada Treasury bills auctioned in the first month of the preceding quarter, rounded up to the next whole percentage point. The July auctions yielded 2.29% on July 14 and 2.29% on July 28, which produced the 3% rate for October to December, according to Advisor.ca.</p><p>The same arithmetic governs the first quarter of 2027, which will be set from October auctions. The Bank of Canada 90-day bill yield was 2.38% on October 13. A move to 4% requires an October average above 3.00%, which is 62 basis points higher. A move down to 2% requires an average at or below 2.00%, which is 38 basis points lower.</p><h2>Why October 28 Cannot Close the Gap</h2><p>The Bank of Canada overnight rate is 2.25% and its next decision is on October 28. Odds of a quarter-point increase fell to about 25% from about 40% after Statistics Canada reported about 68,000 lost jobs in September, according to LSEG data cited by the Financial Post. TD Economics expects a hold, CIBC expects holds at both remaining 2026 meetings, and Capital Economics expects an increase soon to 2.75%, the midpoint of the neutral range. Even the most hawkish of those calls leaves the policy rate below 3.00%.</p><p>Three-month bills have barely reacted to the war-driven inflation story. The 90-day yield has stayed between 2.24% and 2.39% since June 1, and it rose only 4 basis points between the Federal Reserve September 16 hike, when it was 2.34%, and October 13. If bills moved one for one with the policy rate, a single 25 basis point increase would supply about 40% of the 62 basis points required, and it would take roughly two and a half such increases to lift October average above 3.00%.</p><p>Yields have stayed within 15 basis points of 2.30% through the whole period, and the 3.00% line that would lift the rate sits 62 basis points above the latest reading.</p><div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
<div style="background:#f5f5f5;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d0d0;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
<span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#111;letter-spacing:0.02em;">90-DAY GOC T-BILL YIELD</span>
<span style="font-size:20px;font-weight:700;color:#111;">2.38%</span>
<span style="font-size:13px;color:#2e7d32;">▲ 4 BP SINCE SEP 16</span>
<span style="font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-left:auto;">DAILY MID &nbsp;|&nbsp; JUN 1 TO OCT 13, 2026</span>
</div>
<div style="padding:12px 14px 8px;">
