<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Daily yields are Bank of Canada mid-market quotes, so the July average of 2.26% on this series differs from the 2.29% auction average that set the fourth-quarter rate. The dashed green line marks the 3.00% October average that would lift the first-quarter 2027 rate to 4%.</p><h2>What a 3% Loan Does and Does Not Do</h2><p>A prescribed rate loan lets a higher-income spouse or common-law partner, or a family trust, lend money at the prescribed rate in effect on the day the loan is made. The rate stays fixed for the life of the loan as long as the interest is paid each year within 30 days after year-end, which makes January 30, 2027 the deadline for interest on any loan made in 2026. If a payment is late, investment income earned with the borrowed money is attributed back to the lender for that year and every later year, according to Advisor.ca.</p><p>The structure works in taxable accounts held by the lower-income spouse or a family trust. On a $500,000 loan, annual interest at 3% is $15,000, which is income to the lender and deductible to the borrower when the money is invested to earn income, so the borrower portfolio has to earn more than 3% for the structure to produce a spread. Registered accounts follow their own rules and sit outside it. The 2026 TFSA limit is $7,000, according to CRA figures reported by the Globe and Mail.</p><h2>Three Dates and Four Client Segments</h2><p>Three dates matter. October 28 is when both the Federal Reserve and the Bank of Canada announce decisions. December 31 is the last day a loan is certain to keep 3%, because a loan made in 2027 takes the first-quarter rate. January 30, 2027 is the interest deadline for loans made in 2026.</p><p>Clients segment cleanly. Couples with one spouse in a high bracket and one with little taxable income are the core case. Families with an existing trust add a distribution decision. Clients who already hold a prescribed rate loan have a payment to make, not a rate to watch. Retirees already splitting eligible pension income start from a different baseline, so the incremental gain is smaller.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who read the rate headlines are anxious that borrowing costs and tax planning windows are about to close. Those with a family loan in place worry the rate will be reset against them. Those who have meant to set one up feel pressure to act before year-end. Many cannot tell the Bank of Canada rate from the CRA rate and assume a central bank hike on October 28 changes both.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to clear up one worry. The CRA rate on family loans is not the Bank of Canada rate, and it does not move with each central bank decision. It is set from three-month Treasury bill auctions, rounded up to the next whole per cent. Those bills yielded 2.38% on October 13, so they would have to average above 3.00% in October for the rate to move from 3% to 4% in January. That is 62 basis points away, and neither central bank decision on October 28 is likely to close that gap. If you already have a loan, its rate is locked as long as the interest is paid by January 30 each year. If you are thinking about one, there is no reason to rush on the rate alone, but the paperwork and the first interest payment have to be in order, and I would like to confirm both with you in the next few weeks.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> clients with an existing prescribed rate loan or family trust loan, because the interest for 2026 must be paid by January 30, 2027 to avoid attribution for that year and every later year.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> clients planning a new loan, who can expect 3% for the first quarter of 2027 on current data but will not know for certain until the CRA announces it.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> couples with a large income gap and non-registered assets, who can lock 3% for the life of a loan by making it before December 31.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">List every client with an existing prescribed rate loan and confirm the interest amount and payment date for 2026.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Set a reminder to confirm each interest payment was made by January 30, 2027.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients considering a new loan and book discussions before December 31.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm each loan is documented in writing with the correct rate and the lender and borrower named.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Record the CRA announcement of the first-quarter 2027 rate when it is published and compare it with the 3% base case.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Your family loan rate: what the headlines do and do not change<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Thank you for your call. Here is the short version. The CRA rate on family loans is set from three-month Treasury bill auctions, not from the Bank of Canada decision. Those bills yielded 2.38% on October 13, which is 62 basis points below the level needed to lift the rate from 3% to 4% in January.<br><br>
If you already have a loan, the key date is January 30, 2027, when the 2026 interest must be paid. If you are considering a new one, a loan made by December 31 keeps 3% for its life. I will follow up with the paperwork checklist this week.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Higher-income earners with a lower-income spouse and a large non-registered portfolio:</strong> the group for whom a 3% family loan has the largest potential effect.</p>
<p><strong>Families with an existing trust and no loan strategy:</strong> the trust is already in place, so the decision is whether to use it before December 31.</p>
<p><strong>DIY investors who confuse the CRA rate with the Bank of Canada rate:</strong> headlines about hikes may be pushing them to act early or not at all.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">The CRA rate for family loans has been 3% for six quarters, and the October bill data point to a seventh. I am calling people who might still be able to use it before December 31.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The window is long but not open-ended. A loan made by December 31 keeps 3% for its life, and a loan made later takes the first-quarter 2027 rate. On current data that rate is 3% as well, so the gap between acting and waiting is small in expectation and large in the tail.</p>
<p>The asymmetry is in execution. A prospect managing this alone has to document the loan, pay the interest by January 30 each year and track attribution risk. A missed payment attributes the income back to the lender for that year and every later year. The advisor brings the process that prevents that error.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Do you and your spouse or partner have similar taxable incomes, or is there a large gap?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Roughly how much of your investment portfolio is held outside registered accounts?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Do you have a family trust, and if so, has it been used for investing?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Have you looked at a family loan before, and what stopped you?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Who tracks the interest payment date each year?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The 3% family loan rate and the dates that matter<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The CRA rate for family loans has been 3% for six quarters, and three-month Treasury bills sit 62 basis points below the level that would raise it to 4% in January. A loan made by December 31 keeps its rate for life, and the interest is due by January 30 each year.<br><br>
If one spouse has a much higher income and you hold investments outside registered accounts, it may be worth a conversation. Would 15 minutes this week work?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
