<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The dashed red line marks the 2.25% Bank of Canada target for the overnight rate, which did not change over the period. The 5-year yield peaked at 3.69% on September 23 and the 2-year yield at 3.40% on the same day.</p><h2>What October 28 Has to Weigh</h2><p>The Federal Reserve raised its target range to 3.75% to 4.00% on September 16, and the Canadian two-year yield rose from 3.01% on September 1 to 3.35% on September 16. The U.S. 10-year Treasury yield touched a 24-year high on October 5, according to Reuters. The Canadian curve has been responding to U.S. rates and to oil before it has responded to Canadian data.</p><p>For the Bank of Canada the decision is whether to validate a curve that has priced tightening ahead of its own forecasts. Capital Economics expects an increase soon to 2.75%, the midpoint of the neutral range, while TD Economics and CIBC expect the Bank to wait. The October Monetary Policy Report will show which growth and inflation path the Bank is using to choose between them.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Homeowners with a renewal ahead are anxious that mortgage rates are heading higher and that the Bank of Canada is about to make it worse. Clients with bond funds see yields rising and prices falling and wonder what they own. Some conflate the policy rate with the five-year bond yield and expect a hold on October 28 to bring fixed rates down. Savers feel mixed: the higher yields help new money and hurt existing holdings.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Let me separate two things that get mixed together. The Bank of Canada rate is 2.25% and it has not moved since at least March. Fixed mortgage rates follow the five-year government bond yield, and that yield rose from 3.35% on September 1 to 3.69% on September 23 before easing to 3.55%. The bond market is pricing increases that the Bank has not announced. On October 19 we get September inflation and on October 28 we get the Bank decision and its new forecast. I cannot tell you which way the five-year yield goes from here. What I can do is look at your renewal date, your cash flow and your options, so you are choosing from a plan and not from a headline. Can we find 20 minutes this week to walk through it?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> clients with a fixed-rate mortgage renewal in the next 12 months, and clients who plan to buy and have not locked a rate.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> holders of bond funds and fixed-income ETFs, who see lower prices from higher yields along with higher income going forward, and clients with variable-rate debt tied to the policy rate, which has not changed.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> savers adding to GICs or short-term bonds at higher yields, and retirees building income ladders.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">List every client with a mortgage renewal in the next 12 months and note the renewal date.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients with bond fund holdings and check each fund duration against the client time horizon.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Calendar outreach for October 19 and October 28 so calls happen before the releases.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm cash flow for each renewing client under a five-year rate one percentage point above the current one.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document each conversation, including what the client was told about the difference between the policy rate and the bond yield.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Your renewal and what the bond market is doing<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Thank you for talking today. To summarize: the Bank of Canada rate is 2.25%, and fixed mortgage rates follow the five-year government bond yield instead. That yield rose from 3.35% to 3.69% in September and was 3.55% on October 13. September inflation arrives on October 19 and the Bank decision and new forecast arrive on October 28.<br><br>
I will send the renewal cash flow comparison we discussed before October 19. Please call if your timing changes.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Homeowners with a fixed-rate renewal in the next 12 months who are managing it alone:</strong> the five-year yield moved 34 basis points in three weeks in September and they have no one to ask what it means.</p>
<p><strong>DIY investors holding bond funds or GICs:</strong> rising yields have lowered bond prices, and they may be unsure whether to sell, hold or add.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with floating-rate credit:</strong> they follow the policy rate headline and may not see that the curve has already moved.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">The two-year government bond yield is 94 basis points above the Bank of Canada rate, which means the bond market is pricing increases the Bank has not announced. I am calling people with renewals coming up to ask how they are thinking about it.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>Two scheduled events fall inside the next two weeks: Canadian inflation on October 19 and the Bank of Canada decision and Monetary Policy Report on October 28. A prospect with a renewal ahead faces a decision that depends on the five-year bond yield, which has moved more than the policy rate this year, and has no framework for deciding when to lock.</p>
<p>The advisor offers a plan in place of a guess: renewal date, cash flow under several rate outcomes and a way to compare fixed and variable. The self-directed borrower is reacting to headlines. The advisor can show what each outcome costs before the headline arrives.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<div class="checklist-item">When does your mortgage come up for renewal?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">What rate are you paying now, and what have you been quoted?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">How much would your monthly payment have to rise before it changed your plans?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Do you hold bond funds or GICs, and how have you reacted to the move in yields?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Who are you talking to about the decision right now?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The bond market is ahead of the Bank of Canada<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The two-year government bond yield is 94 basis points above the Bank of Canada rate, and the five-year yield that sets fixed mortgage rates rose 34 basis points in September. Canadian inflation arrives on October 19 and the Bank decision on October 28.<br><br>
If you have a renewal coming up, it may help to see what different rate outcomes would mean for your payments before those dates. Would 15 minutes this week work?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '[{"value":"3.19%","label":"Two-year GoC yield"},{"value":"94 bp","label":"Two-year spread to policy rate"},{"value":"3.55%","label":"Five-year GoC yield"},{"value":"4.34%","label":"Lowest five-year fixed, Ratehub"}]',
  'economy-133.jpg',
  'Government of Canada bond yields have repriced Bank of Canada expectations since March, while the overnight rate has stayed at 2.25%. Photo: iStock.',
  6,
  '2026-10-15T08:34:00',
