VALUES (
  '2026/10/15/hormuz-two-transits-a-day-brent-106-canadian-portfolio',
  'geo',
  'article',
  'Two Transits a Day Through Hormuz Against a Baseline of 85: What a US$106 Brent Price Means for a Canadian Portfolio',
  'Reported Iranian attacks on two more ships on October 14 keep the oil risk premium intact, and Canadian energy earnings are the part of the portfolio that collects it.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The strait is effectively shut,</strong><span> IMF PortWatch recorded 2 transits on October 11 against a pre-crisis baseline of 85 a day.</span></li>
<li><strong>Iran reportedly attacked two more vessels on October 14,</strong><span> and Brent briefly topped US$107 in early trading before closing at US$106.06.</span></li>
<li><strong>Canadian producers collect the premium,</strong><span> operating profits rose 68% from Q1 to Q2 2026, according to Deloitte.</span></li>
<li><strong>Supply cannot respond soon,</strong><span> the proposed Pacific Link pipeline targets a regulatory decision by September 1, 2027, and Trans Mountain is full.</span></li>
<li><strong>The tail risk runs both ways,</strong><span> a reopening would take WTI toward the US$76.50 that Deloitte assumes for 2027, 20% below the October 14 close.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>Iran reportedly attacked two commercial vessels in the Strait of Hormuz on October 14 using new tactics, according to Reuters, and Brent crude briefly topped US$107 a barrel in early trading before closing at US$106.06. IMF PortWatch recorded two transits through the strait on October 11, against a pre-crisis baseline of 85 a day. For a Canadian investor the headline matters less than the mechanism: the closure holds a risk premium in the oil price, and that premium reaches a portfolio through energy earnings, through inflation and through the interest rate path at the same time.</p><h2>How the Premium Reaches Canadian Earnings</h2><p>Canadian producers are price takers. They sell a barrel at a price set in global markets, so a supply shock thousands of kilometres away lands directly in their operating profit. Deloitte reports that operating profits at Canadian oil producers rose 68% between the first and second quarters of 2026.</p><p>West Texas Intermediate closed at US$95.09 on October 14, which is 38.7% above its July 1 close of US$68.58 and above the US$90 average that Deloitte projects for 2026. The iShares S&amp;P/TSX Capped Energy ETF closed at $30.55 on October 14, up 6.1% from $28.79 on October 2 and up 27.3% from $24.00 on July 2. A holder of a broad Canadian index owns part of this exposure by default. The same premium raises prices at the pump, which is why Continuum Economics expects Canadian headline inflation to hold at 3.0% when September data arrive on October 19.</p><p>West Texas Intermediate has risen from US$68.58 on July 1 to US$95.09 on October 14, and it now sits above both Deloitte price assumptions, the US$90 average for 2026 and the US$76.50 average for 2027.</p><div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
<div style="background:#f5f5f5;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d0d0;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
<span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#111;letter-spacing:0.02em;">WTI CRUDE, US$ PER BARREL</span>
<span style="font-size:20px;font-weight:700;color:#111;">$95.09</span>
<span style="font-size:13px;color:#2e7d32;">▲ 38.7% SINCE JUL 1</span>
<span style="font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-left:auto;">DAILY CLOSE &nbsp;|&nbsp; JUL 1 TO OCT 14, 2026</span>
</div>
<div style="padding:12px 14px 8px;">
<script>
(function(){
var _cs = document.currentScript;
