</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The dashed lines mark Deloitte average WTI assumptions of US$90 for 2026 and US$76.50 for 2027. WTI peaked at a close of US$105.83 on September 15.</p><h2>Why Supply Cannot Respond Inside the Forecast Window</h2><p>A price this high would normally call forth new supply, and in Canada it is not doing so quickly. Prime Minister Mark Carney announced on October 1 that the proposed Pacific Link pipeline, a 1 million barrel a day export line to the west coast, would be treated as a project of national interest with a single federal review. The target for finishing that review is September 1, 2027, and the estimated cost is C$35.2 billion to C$43.7 billion. The existing Trans Mountain pipeline carries 890,000 barrels a day and is already at capacity.</p><p>Capital spending shows the same caution. Statistics Canada capital expenditures rose about 7% to $11 billion in the second quarter, which Deloitte describes as modest movement, and drilling activity was up 17% from a year earlier according to Baker Hughes data cited by Deloitte. The Canadian gain from the Hormuz closure is therefore a gain in price and not in volume, and it lasts only as long as the premium lasts. Deloitte assumes WTI averages US$76.50 in 2027, which is US$18.59 below the October 14 close. The forecasters are treating the premium as temporary even as the strait stays shut.</p><h2>Base Case and Tail Risks</h2><p>The base case, in the judgment of this desk, is continued stalemate with oil volatile and elevated. Brent closed between US$104.72 and US$106.65 on each session from October 9 to October 14, and WTI has not closed above US$96 since September 18. The market is pricing a closed strait, not an escalating one.</p><p>The first tail risk is escalation beyond shipping. Saudi Arabia said an attack on an Aramco building in the east of the kingdom caused a limited fire, and that debris from intercepted Houthi missiles wounded four people in Riyadh. Damage to Gulf production capacity, as opposed to transit, would be a different shock. European gas is already near a four-year high, with Dutch TTF futures trading just above 86 euros a megawatt hour against a peak of 87.44.</p><p>The second tail risk is a reopening, and for a Canadian portfolio it is the one that is under-discussed. A move to the US$76.50 that Deloitte assumes for 2027 would be a 19.5% fall from the October 14 WTI close. Energy equities that rose 27.3% since July 2 would give back part of that, and the profit growth now supporting Canadian index earnings would shrink with it.</p><h2>What a Canadian Portfolio Is Actually Long</h2><p>The consequence is that a Canadian portfolio is long the war premium in one place and exposed to it in another. Energy earnings benefit from the price. Everything that is sensitive to interest rates pays for it, because oil lifts headline inflation and headline inflation shapes the rate path. The U.S. 10-year Treasury yield was 5.21% on October 13, the Federal Reserve raised its target range to 3.75% to 4.00% on September 16, and both the Fed and the Bank of Canada decide on October 28.</p><p>Three observable items will show which tail is getting closer: the daily transit count at the strait, the September Canadian CPI on October 19, and the Bank of Canada decision on October 28 with its Monetary Policy Report. A rise in transits from two a day would be the first signal of a reopening. A strike on Saudi infrastructure would be the first signal of the opposite.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients see ships being attacked and oil above US$100 and assume the worst for their portfolio. Some are frightened that a larger war is coming. Others notice that their energy holdings are up and wonder whether to sell, add or ignore it. Clients who pay for gasoline and heating feel the cost directly and connect it to the rate worries they already have.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The Strait of Hormuz is almost closed, with two transits recorded on October 11 against a normal 85 a day, and that is why oil is near US$106. For a Canadian portfolio it cuts two ways. Energy companies earn more at this price, and Deloitte reports their operating profits rose 68% from the first to the second quarter. But the same oil price pushes up inflation and interest rates, which hurts other holdings. I cannot tell you whether the strait reopens next week or next year. What I can do is show you how much of your portfolio benefits from high oil and how much is exposed to it, so we can decide whether that balance is the one you want. Can we look at it together this week?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> clients with concentrated energy positions or a TSX-heavy portfolio, who hold the premium and would feel a reopening.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> balanced investors holding both energy and rate-sensitive sectors such as utilities, real estate and financials.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> clients who are underweight energy and hold cash, who gain flexibility if a reopening or an escalation creates a better entry point.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Calculate each client energy exposure across direct holdings, funds and the Canadian index weighting.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag clients whose energy gains since July now exceed their target allocation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review rate-sensitive holdings for clients with renewals or leverage ahead of October 28.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare talking points for the two tail scenarios, reopening and escalation, before October 19.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document the rebalancing conversation and the rationale for any decision to hold.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Oil, Hormuz and what it means for your portfolio<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Thank you for talking today. To summarize: the Strait of Hormuz remains almost closed, oil is near US$106, and Canadian energy companies are benefiting while rate-sensitive holdings face pressure from inflation and interest rates.<br><br>
I will send you a breakdown of how much of your portfolio sits on each side before October 19, when Canadian inflation data are released. Please call if you would like to talk sooner.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors with a TSX index fund as their core holding:</strong> they own a large energy weighting without having chosen it and may not know how much of their return depends on oil.</p>
<p><strong>Investors who bought energy stocks since July:</strong> they have a large gain and no plan for what happens if the strait reopens.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners in transport, farming or manufacturing:</strong> they pay the cost of high oil directly and hold no offsetting exposure.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Oil is near US$106 because the Strait of Hormuz is nearly shut, and Canadian energy stocks have risen about 27% since early July. I am calling people who own the Canadian index to ask whether they know how much of their return is riding on that one price.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The oil price is held up by a risk premium that could fall by a fifth if the strait reopens, or rise if the conflict spreads. A prospect with a concentrated Canadian portfolio carries that risk without a plan for either outcome.</p>
<p>The advisor offers an exposure review and two defined scenarios, so the client can act on a decision made in advance and not on a headline. The self-directed investor is reacting after the move has happened.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<div class="checklist-item">How much of your portfolio is in Canadian energy, directly or through funds?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">What would you do if oil fell 20% in a month?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Have you added to energy since the summer, and was that a decision or a reaction?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">How much would higher interest rates affect the rest of your holdings or your debt?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Who is helping you decide whether to hold, trim or add?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
