VALUES (
  '2026/10/15/tsx-down-1-9-percent-energy-up-gold-miners-down',
  'market',
  'article',
  'The TSX Is Down 1.9% Since August 31, but Energy Is Up 7.5% and Gold Miners Are Down 13.1%',
  'One oil shock is working through the Canadian index in opposite directions, and the rate channel is what separates the winners from the losers.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The index hides a 20.6 point gap,</strong><span> XEG is up 7.5% since August 31 and XGD is down 13.1%, against a 1.9% decline for the S&amp;P/TSX Composite.</span></li>
<li><strong>Gold is falling with the rate path,</strong><span> the U.S. 10-year yield rose 43 basis points to 5.19% since August 31 while gold fell 8.3%.</span></li>
<li><strong>October 14 was a rate day,</strong><span> the TSX fell 1.1%, XGD fell 4.2%, gold fell 1.7% and XEG was unchanged.</span></li>
<li><strong>Financials are not the story,</strong><span> XFN is down 1.7%, in line with the index.</span></li>
<li><strong>Two dates decide the next leg,</strong><span> Canadian CPI on October 19 and the Bank of Canada and Fed decisions on October 28.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The S&amp;P/TSX Composite closed at 35,584.46 on October 14, down 1.9% from its August 31 close of 36,270.50, but the index hides a 20.6 percentage point gap underneath. The iShares energy ETF (XEG) is up 7.5% over that period and the iShares gold miners ETF (XGD) is down 13.1%. The two sectors are reacting to the same oil shock in opposite directions, and the rate path is the channel that separates them.</p><h2>The Rate Channel Splits the Index</h2><p>Oil near US$106 lifts energy earnings directly. It also lifts inflation, and inflation has pushed the Federal Reserve to raise its target range to 3.75% to 4.00% on September 16. The U.S. 10-year Treasury yield closed at 5.19% on October 14, up 43 basis points from 4.76% on August 31. Gold pays no yield, so a higher yield raises the cost of holding it. Gold fell from US$4,481.50 to US$4,107.90 over the period, a decline of 8.3%.</p><p>Gold miners fell 13.1% against that 8.3% decline in the metal, about 1.6 times as far, because costs are fixed in the short run and a lower gold price comes straight out of margins. XGD closed at $52.83 on October 14, its lowest close of the period and 14.9% below its September 3 close of $62.06.</p><p>XGD has fallen 13.1% while XEG has risen 7.5% since August 31, and the gap is the rate channel at work in a single index.</p><div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
