<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Yahoo Finance daily closes for the S&amp;P/TSX Composite (^GSPTSE), XEG.TO, XGD.TO and XFN.TO, Aug 31 to Oct 14, 2026. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">XEG is the iShares S&amp;P/TSX Capped Energy ETF, XGD the iShares S&amp;P/TSX Global Gold ETF and XFN the iShares S&amp;P/TSX Capped Financials ETF. Each series is rebased to 100 at its August 31 close, shown by the dashed line, and sessions without a close in all four series are excluded.</p><h2>October 14 Was a Rate Day</h2><p>The most recent session shows the split at full strength. U.S. consumer prices released on October 14 came in hotter than expected, with headline inflation at 3.7% and the core monthly gain at 0.3% against 0.2% expected, and the S&amp;P/TSX Composite fell 1.1%, or about 400 points, to 35,584.46. The S&amp;P 500 fell 1.1% to 7,718.94 and the VIX rose to 16.87 from 15.27.</p><p>Inside the Canadian index the damage was concentrated. XGD fell 4.2% and gold fell 1.7%. XFN fell 0.5%. XEG closed unchanged at $30.55 as the oil price held near its highs, with Brent closing at US$106.06. The gold miners moved about 2.5 times as far as the metal, which fell 1.7%. The 10-year yield closed at 5.19%, 3 basis points below the prior day, so the split reflects a repricing of the Fed path and not a move in the long end alone. A day when the index fell 1.1% therefore contained a 4.2% decline in one sector and no decline in another. Anyone reading only the index saw a broad sell-off. The sector tape shows a sell-off in the assets most sensitive to the U.S. yield.</p><h2>What Decides the Next Leg</h2><p>Financials have gone nowhere, which matters for what the index tells you. XFN is down 1.7% since August 31, in line with the 1.9% fall in the TSX, so the move is not a banking story. It is the net of a 7.5% gain in energy and a 13.1% loss in gold.</p><p>Two dates will test the spread. Statistics Canada reports September CPI on October 19, and Continuum Economics expects headline inflation to hold at 3.0%. The Fed and the Bank of Canada both decide on October 28. The odds of a Fed increase were roughly 42% to 50% on October 14 depending on the source, while LSEG put the odds of a Bank of Canada increase at about 25% after the September jobs loss of roughly 68,000.</p><p>The implication is that the spread is the position. A hot print and a hike would extend the pattern, with energy holding and gold miners under pressure. A soft print would relieve the rate channel first, which would help gold miners before it did anything for energy. A reopening of the Strait of Hormuz would hit the energy leg directly.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with gold miners see a double-digit loss while the headlines talk about a world on edge, and they ask why gold is falling when the news is bad. Clients with energy see gains and wonder whether to take them. Clients holding the index see a small decline and feel nothing, without knowing what is underneath it.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The Canadian index is down about 2% since the end of August, but that number hides a big split. Energy is up 7.5% and gold miners are down 13.1%. Both are reacting to the same thing: oil is high, inflation is high, interest rates are rising and the U.S. 10-year yield is 5.19%. Gold pays no income, so higher yields work against it. That is why gold has fallen even with bad news in the world. I cannot tell you which way it goes from here. Canadian inflation comes out on October 19 and both central banks decide on October 28. What I can do is show you how much of your portfolio sits on each side of this split, so we can decide whether that is the balance you want.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> clients with concentrated gold miner or precious metals holdings, who have borne the loss.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> clients holding the broad Canadian index, where energy gains and gold losses largely offset.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> clients with energy gains above target weight who may want to rebalance, and clients with cash who see lower prices in gold miners.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients with more than 10% of their portfolio in gold miners or precious metals funds.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients whose energy weight has drifted above target since August 31.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review the tax position on any rebalancing sale before recommending it.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Calendar outreach for October 19 and October 28, ahead of the CPI release and the central bank decisions.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document each conversation, including what the client was told about the rate sensitivity of gold.</div>
</div>
